From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan <ryan@esterlines.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 14:50:34 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] RequestServer+LibWebView: Let the user accept a certificate
 that fails verification

Browsers let their users past a certificate error for a site they know,
like a server of their own with a self-signed certificate. RequestServer
now tells the UI process which certificate it rejected, DER-encoded, so
that a browser can show the user why and offer to accept it. curl only
collects the server's certificates when asked to before it connects, so
a request whose server's certificate fails verification is made once
more with CURLOPT_CERTINFO. Its handshake failed before anything was
sent, so nothing is sent twice.

RequestServerManager::add_certificate_exception() accepts a certificate
for a host and port. The session keeps it, and each of its RequestServers
takes it, those launched later too. Requests to the host and port,
WebSockets included, stop verifying the server's certificate and have
curl pin the accepted certificate's public key instead, so no other
certificate is accepted in its place. A request that meets another key
is verified as usual. A response over such a connection doesn't make its
host a known HSTS host, as it came with a certificate error.

RequestServerManager::on_server_certificate_rejected tells the embedder
of each rejection.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
